Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Tanzila Naseem
Title: Community Perceptions of the China-Pakistan Economic Corridor: Implications for Economic, Social, and Sustainable Development
Abstract: The China-Pakistan Economic Corridor (CPEC) is a flagship infrastructure initiative designed to enhance regional connectivity, economic development, and sustainable growth. This study examines how community perceptions of CPEC relate to perceived economic development (PED) and perceived societal development (PSD), and how these perceptions are associated with sustainable development and support for CPEC. Drawing on Social Exchange Theory, the study adopts a mixed-methods approach, combining survey data from 100 respondents with qualitative interviews from 15 participants. The findings indicate that perceived economic development and perceived societal development are significant predictors of support for CPEC.
