Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Navya Kedia
Title: An analysis of the effectiveness of the Hong Kong Dollar (HKD) peg to the US Dollar (USD) during crisis periods
Abstract: This paper examines whether Hong Kong's currency peg to the US dollar amplifies economic contractions during crisis periods. Eight countries with varying exchange rate regimes were compared using panel data. A Fixed Effects panel regression model with a PEG x Crisis interaction term was used to test whether pegged economies experience larger GDP declines than non-pegged economies specifically during crisis years. The findings suggest that pegged economies may suffer more during crisis periods, with an additional 3.3 percentage point decline in GDP growth (p = 0.093). Hong Kong faces a trade-off between the stability of the peg and a lack of independent monetary policy actions.
